      <p>
        The benchmark results demonstrate clear domain specialization. Cohere Embed v3 exhibits a statistically significant advantage in <strong>financial tabular retrieval (FiQA: +2.5 pts)</strong> and <strong>technical software documentation (CQADupStack: +2.1 pts)</strong>. This advantage is largely attributable to Cohere's asymmetric projection mechanism, which maps concise questions to verbose descriptive passages far more effectively than symmetric vector spaces. Conversely, OpenAI text-embedding-3-large maintains an advantage in scientific terminology (SciFact: +1.2 pts) and conversational argumentation (Touche-2020: +1.6 pts), reflecting OpenAI's massive pretraining corpus.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Vector Database RAM Sizing &amp; HNSW Memory Mathematics (1M to 100M Vectors)
      </h2>
      <p>
        In high-throughput vector search architectures (e.g. pgvector, Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate, Pinecone), Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs require that both raw vectors and graph adjacency links reside entirely within physical RAM to sustain low-latency nearest-neighbor traversal.
      </p>
      <p>
        The total RAM requirement M_total for an index containing N vectors of dimensionality D with quantization bit-depth b, HNSW construction parameter M (links per node), and 64-bit integer IDs is calculated by:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <code>M_total = N * [ (D * b / 8) + (M * 2 * 8 bytes) + 16 bytes overhead ]</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        For a standard production HNSW configuration with $M = 16$ bi-directional links (32 connection pointers per node):
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>OpenAI 3-Large (3072 dimensions, float32)</strong>: <code>3072 * 4 bytes = 12,288 bytes</code> per vector. Adding HNSW graph link overhead (~3,200 bytes per node), 1M vectors consume <strong>15.48 GB of RAM</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Cohere Embed v3 (1024 dimensions, float32)</strong>: <code>1024 * 4 bytes = 4,096 bytes</code> per vector. With graph links, 1M vectors consume <strong>5.19 GB of RAM</strong> (a 66.5% reduction vs OpenAI).</li>
        <li><strong>Cohere Embed v3 (1024 dimensions, int8)</strong>: <code>1024 * 1 byte = 1,024 bytes</code> per vector. With compressed HNSW index, 1M vectors consume just <strong>1.37 GB of RAM</strong> (a 91.1% reduction vs OpenAI float32).</li>
        <li><strong>Cohere Embed v3 (1024 dimensions, binary)</strong>: <code>1024 / 8 bytes = 128 bytes</code> per vector. With binary inverted index, 1M vectors consume an astonishing <strong>0.18 GB of RAM</strong> (a 98.8% reduction).</li>
      </ul>

