      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Native Int8 &amp; Binary Compression in Cohere vs Matryoshka Truncation in OpenAI
      </h2>
      <p>
        How do these two models compress vector data without catastrophic loss of retrieval fidelity? Their underlying mathematical mechanisms operate across completely distinct dimensions of linear algebra:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Cohere: Quantization-Aware Training</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Cohere Embed v3 was trained with quantization-aware objectives. Instead of relying on post-training scalar quantization, the neural network's loss function penalizes rounding error when mapped to signed 8-bit integers ([-128, 127]) or single-bit binary thresholds (&gt;0 $\rightarrow$ 1, $\le$0 $\rightarrow$ 0). As a result, int8 embeddings preserve <strong>99.9% of full float32 NDCG@10</strong>, and binary embeddings retain <strong>96.5% of accuracy</strong> when paired with two-phase rescoring.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-2">OpenAI: Matryoshka Representation Learning</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            OpenAI text-embedding-3-large leverages Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL, Kusupati et al.). During training, the loss function is evaluated simultaneously across multiple nested sub-vector prefix lengths: d in [256, 512, 1024, 3072]. The model forces the most critical semantic variance into early vector indices. Developers can simply slice the first K dimensions and apply L2 normalization: <code>v_norm = v[:1024] / np.linalg.norm(v[:1024])</code>, preserving <strong>98.6% of accuracy</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Production Python Implementation: Asymmetric Embeddings &amp; Two-Stage Rescoring Pipeline
      </h2>
      <p>
        The following copyable, production-ready Python script implements asymmetric embedding ingestion, native int8 retrieval, and two-stage binary Hamming distance filtering with float rescoring:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>import os
import numpy as np
import cohere
from openai import OpenAI
from typing import List, Dict, Any

# Initialize production API clients
COHERE_KEY = os.getenv("COHERE_API_KEY", "your-cohere-key")
OPENAI_KEY = os.getenv("OPENAI_API_KEY", "your-openai-key")

co = cohere.Client(COHERE_KEY)
ai = OpenAI(api_key=OPENAI_KEY)

corpus_documents: List[str] = [
    "PostgreSQL 17 incorporates accelerated B-Tree bulk loading and parallel GIN builds.",
    "Litestream streams SQLite WAL frames continuously to S3-compatible cloud storage.",
    "Qdrant implements binary quantization with Hamming distance SIMD AVX-512 kernels.",
    "Cohere Embed v3 uses compression-aware training to minimize int8 precision degradation.",
    "OpenAI Matryoshka embeddings allow prefix truncation down to 256 dimensions with L2 norm."
]

user_query: str = "How does SQLite perform continuous backup to S3 storage?"

def generate_cohere_production_embeddings(docs: List[str], query: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """
    Generates asymmetric Cohere v3 embeddings in both int8 and binary formats.
    Ensures input_type='search_document' for corpus and 'search_query' for prompt.
    """
    doc_response = co.embed(
        texts=docs,
        model="embed-english-v3.0",
        input_type="search_document",
        embedding_types=["int8", "ubinary"]
    )
    
    query_response = co.embed(
        texts=[query],
        model="embed-english-v3.0",
        input_type="search_query",
        embedding_types=["int8", "ubinary"]
    )
    
    return {
        "doc_int8": np.array(doc_response.embeddings.int8, dtype=np.int8),
        "doc_ubinary": np.array(doc_response.embeddings.ubinary, dtype=np.uint8),
        "query_int8": np.array(query_response.embeddings.int8[0], dtype=np.int8),
        "query_ubinary": np.array(query_response.embeddings.ubinary[0], dtype=np.uint8)
    }

def generate_openai_matryoshka_embeddings(docs: List[str], target_dims: int = 1024) -> np.ndarray:
    """
    Generates OpenAI text-embedding-3-large embeddings with Matryoshka dimension truncation.
    """
    response = ai.embeddings.create(
        model="text-embedding-3-large",
        input=docs,
        dimensions=target_dims
    )
    vectors = np.array([item.embedding for item in response.data], dtype=np.float32)
    # L2-normalization check
    norms = np.linalg.norm(vectors, axis=1, keepdims=True)
    return vectors / norms

def two_stage_binary_rescore(cohere_data: Dict[str, Any], top_k: int = 2) -> List[int]:
    """
    Stage 1: Fast bitwise XOR Hamming distance ranking on 1-bit packed binary vectors.
    Stage 2: Exact int8 dot-product rescoring on the top candidates.
    """
    query_bin = cohere_data["query_ubinary"]
    doc_bins = cohere_data["doc_ubinary"]
    
    # Fast bitwise Hamming distance computation
    xor_bits = np.bitwise_xor(doc_bins, query_bin)
    hamming_distances = np.sum(np.unpackbits(xor_bits, axis=1), axis=1)
    
    # Retrieve top 4 candidates via Hamming distance
    preliminary_top = np.argsort(hamming_distances)[:4]
    
    # Stage 2: Rescore top 4 using int8 dot product
    query_int8 = cohere_data["query_int8"].astype(np.float32)
    doc_candidates_int8 = cohere_data["doc_int8"][preliminary_top].astype(np.float32)
    
    scores = np.dot(doc_candidates_int8, query_int8)
    final_ranking_local = np.argsort(scores)[::-1]
    
    return [preliminary_top[idx] for idx in final_ranking_local[:top_k]]

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print("[*] Generating Cohere Asymmetric Embeddings...")
    co_data = generate_cohere_production_embeddings(corpus_documents, user_query)
    best_doc_indices = two_stage_binary_rescore(co_data, top_k=2)
    
    print(f"[+] Top Match via Cohere Two-Stage: {corpus_documents[best_doc_indices[0]]}")
    
    print("[*] Generating OpenAI 1024-dim Matryoshka Embeddings...")
    openai_vectors = generate_openai_matryoshka_embeddings(corpus_documents, target_dims=1024)
    print(f"[+] OpenAI Vector Shape: {openai_vectors.shape} (Successfully truncated from 3072)")
</code></pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Hybrid Search Architecture: Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) with Sparse BM25
      </h2>
      <p>
        In enterprise information systems, pure dense semantic retrieval frequently fails on alphanumeric part numbers, compliance acronyms (e.g. <code>SOC2-Type-II</code>, <code>CVE-2026-4401</code>), and exact SKU queries. The optimal production architecture fuses dense vector results with sparse lexical BM25 rankings via <strong>Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The RRF score for document $d$ across search rankings $R$ with smoothing parameter $k = 60$ is defined as:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <code>RRF_Score(d) = SUM [ 1.0 / (60 + rank_m(d)) ] across all search rankers m</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        Where rank_m(d) represents the 1-based rank position of document d within retrieval system m in [BM25, Dense Vector]. Below is the high-performance PostgreSQL <code>pgvector</code> query combining full-text search with Cohere 1024-dim cosine distance:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>-- PostgreSQL 17 Hybrid Search: Reciprocal Rank Fusion (k=60)
WITH lexical_ranking AS (
  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ts_rank_cd(tsv_content, plainto_tsquery('english', 'SQLite S3 replication')) DESC) as rank
  FROM enterprise_kb
  WHERE tsv_content @@ plainto_tsquery('english', 'SQLite S3 replication')
  LIMIT 50
),
vector_ranking AS (
  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY embedding_1024 &lt;=&gt; '[0.012,-0.045,...]'::vector(1024)) as rank
  FROM enterprise_kb
  LIMIT 50
)
SELECT 
  COALESCE(l.id, v.id) as doc_id,
  COALESCE(1.0 / (60 + l.rank), 0.0) + COALESCE(1.0 / (60 + v.rank), 0.0) AS composite_rrf_score
FROM lexical_ranking l
FULL OUTER JOIN vector_ranking v ON l.id = v.id
ORDER BY composite_rrf_score DESC
LIMIT 10;
</code></pre>

